+## 05 — THE WHITE DRAGON (continuity sheet, anchored on Volume II's approved 05)
+
+A character reference sheet, 1536 × 1024 landscape, showing exactly four
+views of the same dragon on one uninterrupted plain warm-paper field. No
+lettering, labels, borders, scenery, ground line, or environmental vignette.
+Use the attached approved Volume II `05-dragon` sheet as the sole identity
+anchor and keep the character young and unchanged.
+
+The Third Prince in dragon form is a slim white serpentine dragon, long and
+lithe, with a complete tapering tail and four small clawed legs. His face is
+short, narrow, and young: large round sea-green eyes, a short reptilian mouth,
+two small unbranched pale-gold horn spikes, and no beard or jaw fins. A
+pale-blue mane begins at the crown and runs along the spine. At rest he looks
+quick, brave, and a little frightened. He wears no costume or tack.
+
+Arrange four cleanly separated poses without frames: (a) a three-quarter head
+and neck, (b) the complete dragon in a long readable S-curve, (c) a strict
+profile head, and (d) the complete dragon recoiling in startled pain. In view
+(d), turn his anatomical right side toward the viewer and lift the right
+foreleg alone into the foreground, clearly separated from the chest. Put one
+short, straight, closed charcoal-dark gash on the front of that foreleg below
+the knee, wholly inside the white scales with clear unmarked white at both
+ends.
+
+Never give the wound a second stroke, red tint, blood, open flesh, bandage,
+wrap, ring, or dangling material. Never draw horse anatomy, hooves, bridle,
+reins, saddle, or stirrups. Never draw antlers, whiskers, a fin beard,
+blue-green scales, gold eyes, robes, a crown, or a human body.
 
